.Dd November 21, 2026
.Dt NVIDIA_LINUX 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvidia_linux ,
.Nm nvidia_modeset_linux
.Nd Linux compatibility providers of the NVIDIA GPU driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the providers at boot, add to
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
nvidia_linux_load="YES"
nvidia_modeset_linux_load="YES"
.Ed
.Pp
or, in
.Xr rc.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kld_list="nvidia nvidia_modeset nvidia_linux nvidia_modeset_linux"
nvidia_linux_mode="AUTO"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr nvidia 4
and the display module
.Pa nvidia-modeset.ko
load and work with no Linux layer: no
.Pa linux64.ko ,
no
.Pa linux_common.ko
and no
.Pa /compat/linux .
These two modules are the part that Linux binaries need, and they belong to the
Linuxulator.
They are present only when they are loaded.
The unified-memory module
.Pa nvidia-uvm.ko
still depends on the Linuxulator itself until its own provider is split out.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Nm nvidia_linux
Translates the Linux NVIDIA ioctl numbers
.Pq 0x4600 to 0x46ff
into the native encoding, and tells the Linuxulator the device names and the
major and minor numbers of
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl
and
.Pa /dev/nvidia Ns Ar N ,
so that
.Xr stat 2
and
.Pa /proc
show them to Linux programs.
The ioctl handler answers only for descriptors that are one of these devices;
for any other descriptor it returns
.Er ENOIOCTL
and the Linuxulator carries on with its other handlers.
It uses the compat provider interface that
.Xr nvidia 4
exports, and is told about each device as it appears.
It may be loaded before or after the devices exist.
.It Nm nvidia_modeset_linux
Does the same for the display ioctl of
.Pa nvidia-modeset.ko
and for the
.Pa nvidia-modeset
device name.
.El
.Pp
Both depend on the native module they serve and on
.Dv linux64elf
and
.Dv linux_common ,
so loading one loads the Linuxulator.
.Sh LOADING
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d/nvidia_linux
Loads the providers when the Linuxulator is configured.
.Va nvidia_linux_mode
is one of:
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Cm AUTO
the default; load a provider when
.Va linux_enable
is
.Cm YES
and the native module it bridges is already loaded
.It Cm YES
always load them
.It Cm NO
never load them
.El
.El
.Pp
A host with
.Va linux_enable
off and no provider loaded has no Linux code in its GPU stack.
The old behaviour, in which loading
.Xr nvidia 4
loaded the Linuxulator, is reachable by building the core with
.Ev NV_CORE_LINUX_COMPAT Ns = Ns Cm yes
and
.Ev NVKMS_CORE_LINUX_COMPAT Ns = Ns Cm yes ,
or by listing the providers in
.Va kld_list .
.Sh UNLOADING
The providers depend on the native modules, so
.Xr kldunload 8
of a native module is refused
.Pq Er EBUSY ,
and the module stays loaded, while its provider is loaded.
.Xr kldunload 8
takes the file name
.Pq Pa nvidia-modeset ,
not the module name.
Unload in the reverse order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kldunload nvidia_modeset_linux
kldunload nvidia_linux
kldunload nvidia-modeset
kldunload nvidia
.Ed
.Pp
Unloading a provider waits for any Linux ioctl that is inside its handler, as the
Linuxulator holds its handler lock while it calls one.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It Pa /boot/modules/nvidia_linux.ko
.It Pa /boot/modules/nvidia_modeset_linux.ko
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d/nvidia_linux
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr linux 4 ,
.Xr nvidia 4 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr kldunload 8
